      "original": "The fixed background target is the literal finite product gamma_n=Measure.pi (fun i:Fin n => gaussianReal 0 1), a real signed f, Phi(s)=s*Real.log s, and D_i f(x)=fderiv R f x(Pi.single i 1). Entropy is homogeneous: integral Phi(f^2)-Phi(integral f^2). Printed FIRST4.6 concerns the normalized standardized RGO law r_y against standard Gaussian, not this standalone product function theorem.",
      "reconstructed": "An arbitrary n ∈ ℕ; a signed function f : (Fin n → ℝ) → ℝ; coordinate directions e_i = Pi.single i 1 for i ∈ Fin n; derivatives D_i f(x) = fderiv ℝ f x e_i; the square mass A = ∫ f(x)² dγ. γ = Measure.pi (fun _ : Fin n => gaussianReal 0 1), the actual product probability measure of independent centered scalar Gaussian laws with variance one. For n = 0 it is the probability law of the singleton. Every displayed integral is a real Bochner integral with respect to γ. fderiv ℝ f x is the real Fréchet derivative. It is evaluated at each literal coordinate direction Pi.single i 1. The energy is Σ_{i ∈ Fin n} (D_i f(x))², an empty sum equal to zero when n = 0; it is not norm(fderiv ℝ f x)² for the default Pi supremum norm. Real.log is totalized and Real.log 0 = 0.",
